<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ceawlin of Wessex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 593</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ceawlin was a king of the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> in the later sixth century, listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> as the second of the kings who held authority over the southern English. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> credits him with victories over the Britons, notably at Dyrham in 577, which brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirencester">Cirencester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> under West Saxon control. He was driven out after a battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woden">Woden</a>'s Barrow in 592 and died the following year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ceawlin-of-wessex.jpg" alt="Ceawlin of Wessex" width="105" height="42" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mike Christie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ceawlin_name.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
